my name is Alexandra I'm from the  Netherlands I used to work in the press  office until very recently and I worked  a lot with our trainees because I was  responsible for social media and I  always needed extra hands especially  during summits so normally I would have  let's say 2 to 3 trainees helping me but  then during a summit it could be up to  10 I think what makes a great trainee is  a willingness to learn really an  openness happy to try new things happy  to take on extra work  I like trainees who are not afraid to to  step out of their comfort zone and to  try things even though they haven't done  them before and I think that's also what  makes a traineeship a great learning  experience is that you get to do stuff  that you might not be familiar with  stuff that you didn't do it University  and that way you can actually as a  trainee get some really valuable  experience that is useful to you in the  rest of your career what I've seen  happen to quite a few trainees is that  they came here may be relatively  inexperienced or used to working in an  academic setting and then they left with  a much greater sense of what it's like  to you know work in an office context  work also in in a European context and  even in a in a political context because  here at the council we are so close to  European politics this is where even  leaders meet and I've seen Chinese  really grow in that sense maybe we  become more professional or just faster  in completing their tasks and also some  of them have become more confident I  really love how enthusiastic our  trainees are I do remember occasions  when they were maybe a bit too  enthusiastic our current trainees belong  to the generation of Millennials of  digital natives so for them taking a  selfie is normal and sometimes they have  to be reminded that you can't always  take a selfie everywhere and not all  world leaders might appreciate you  taking a selfie with them but other than  that it's yeah it's pretty great working  with these young people
 

